            "reviews": {
                "query_summary": {
                    "num_reviews": 1,
                    "review_score": 0,
                    "review_score_desc": "9 user reviews",
                    "total_positive": 3,
                    "total_negative": 6,
                    "total_reviews": 9
                },
                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "48686092",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198019466684",
                            "num_reviews": 784,
                            "playtime_forever": 50,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 46,
                            "last_played": 1558330200
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[table][th]\nReview by Sven Evil - Steam's best local 4-player curator! See 100s of more local 4-player reviews at my [url=http://store.steampowered.com/curator/8485829-SvenEvils-Playground/] curator page[/url]! \n[/th][/table]\n\nAstro Joust is cheap, really cheap. So you should not judge it too harsh. Its like my english - I am not a native speaker.\n\nAstro Joust is a competititve combat game for 2 to 4 players (no bots!) and ran fine on my system, my XBox 360 and XBox One controllers worked perfectly... but... there is just not much content in the game!\n\nThe game is inspired by Joust, an old arcade classic from 1982, where knights flying on an ostrich were duelling each other. You had to tap the button continuously to keep the ostrich in the air. In Astro Joust you have to tap the button continuously to keep your astronaut in the air. You can also use a button to dash.\n\nJoust had a funny screen wrap feature - fly out on the right side of the screen and reappear on the left. Astro Joust does not have this. In Joust the knights were using long lances, the players in Astro Joust look like they are using power gloves... in Joust you could speed around horizontally like crazy, in Astro Joust you mostly go up and down and try to jump on your opponent's head... not much speed involved, even when you dash.\n\nThe only game mode is Deathmatch - the first player to win 3 rounds wins. That's all. There are 5 different maps to play - that's all. The achievements are extremely unimaginative, the sound is awful. The options menu is very limited, you cannot change key bindings, only choose between 3 resolutions (no FullHD, btw) and not much more.\n\nAstro Joust does come with one innovative feature - its after-death mechanic. Dead players can take over things in the environement and attack the living. Joust can be a little fun when playing with 3 friends, as long as everyone understands the mechanics... but this is simply not enough that I can recommend this game when there are dozens of better platform combat games out there...\n\n[u][b] Conclusion:[/b][/u]\nYou can spend a buck for worse things.",
